  "scriptId": "src-03-square-roots-long-division-approximation",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Square Roots by Long Division and Approximation",
    "description": "Read digits in pairs and build a square root one digit at a time, including decimal and approximate roots.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the long-division algorithm from the meaning of two-digit periods, then move through a two-digit root, a zero-middle root, a three-digit root, exact decimal roots, tiny decimals, approximations and a large exact-decimal capstone. The learner always sees what the trial step is doing and why the decimal point lands where it does.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach the chapter long-division square-root method without relying on paper-only instructions. Read digits in two-digit periods from the units side; choose the largest first square; carry the remainder into the next period; double the root found so far and choose the next digit that fits. Include a case with a zero inside the root, a three-digit exact root, decimal periods such as 1.5625, tiny decimals such as 0.000441, and a non-perfect root rounded to three decimal places. Teach the chapter fact that the number of root digits equals the number of periods for a perfect square. End with an exact decimal capstone. Every approximation must be labelled as approximate.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 31"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the square-root method for numbers that are too awkward to recognise at sight.\n\nThe key idea is simple: read the number in two-digit periods, and each period gives one new digit of the root.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Long division begins by reading the number in two-digit periods from the right.\n\n53,824 becomes:\n5 | 38 | 24",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Each period contributes one digit to the square root.\n\nThree periods → a three-digit square root.\n\nThat is why a 12-digit perfect square has a 6-digit root.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
